{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>To a foreign people he shall not have power to sell her</b> — he is not permitted to sell her to another, neither the master nor the father. <b>If he has dealt deceitfully with her</b> — if he comes to deal deceitfully with her by not fulfilling the commandment of designation (yi'ud), and likewise her father who dealt deceitfully and sold her to this one — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 21:8). If so, לְעַם נָכְרִי would mean the same as לְאִישׁ נָכְרִי, and we find no parallel for it in all of Scripture. But perhaps לְעַם נָכְרִי is to be read as connected: לְעַם — <b>לְנָכְרִי לֹא יִמְשֹׁל לְמָכְרָהּ</b>, and its meaning is like \"you give it as food לְעָם לְצִיִּים\" (Psalms 74:14), where it specifies who the people are — the men of the desert — so too it says <b>לֹא יִמְשֹׁל לְמָכְרָהּ לְעַם</b>, and specifies <b>לְנָכְרִי</b>, meaning to a foreign man from any people, and נָכְרִי would be like \"and your bed in a נָכְרִי house\" (Proverbs 5:10) — in the house of another man; \"her נָכְרִי speech is smooth\" (ibid. 2:16) — she who is not his wife. And all this is to establish the words of the Sages (Kiddushin 19a) who say that a person is not permitted to sell his daughter into servitude after servitude, and they said (there): once he has dealt deceitfully with her, he may no longer sell her.\nBut I saw in the Mekhilta (here): <b>To a foreign people he shall not have power to sell her</b> — this is a warning to the court that they not sell her to a foreigner. And from their language it appears that it is not a warning against selling her a second time, neither to this one nor to another, but rather an absolute prohibition against a foreigner — that a person may not sell his daughter into servitude to a gentile, because since it says regarding a Hebrew slave, \"he shall be sold to a ger toshav who resides with you or to the family of a ger\" (Leviticus 25:47), it was necessary to state that one may not do so with a woman. And the reason is known. And this is certainly the plain sense of Scripture, which states that after the redeeming father has sold her, he may not sell her to a gentile, and the same applies from the outset. But sometimes a person very much wants to redeem his daughter from the master who has not designated her, and wishes to sell her to a gentile for a year or two to get her out of his possession, and Scripture warned against this.\nAlternatively, it returns to the beginning of the matter: <b>If a man sells his daughter as a maidservant, he shall not have power to sell her to a foreigner</b>. If so, that which they derived in the Talmud (Kiddushin 18a) — that a person may not sell his daughter into servitude after servitude — is derived from the superfluity of <b>בְּבִגְדוֹ בָהּ</b>, for to a foreign people he is never permitted to sell her, and if so, why does it say <b>בְּבִגְדוֹ בָהּ</b>, which implies to say: since he dealt deceitfully with her once and sold her into servitude, he may not deal deceitfully with her a second time — therefore they expound the verse thus: <b>לְעַם נָכְרִי לֹא יִמְשֹׁל לְמָכְרָהּ</b> and <b>בְּבִגְדוֹ בָהּ</b>, meaning: and he shall not have power to sell her — <b>בְּבִגְדוֹ בָהּ</b> — since once he has sold her, he may not sell her again. And in many places they expound similarly — read it thus: <b>לַגֵּר אֲשֶׁר בִּשְׁעָרֶיךָ תִּתְּנֶנָּה וַאֲכָלָהּ אוֹ מָכֹר</b> (Deuteronomy 14:21) — תִּתְּנֶנָּה וַאֲכָלָהּ אוֹ מָכֹר לְנָכְרִי (Pesachim 21b). And so they expound here: <b>and she shall go out free, without money</b> (Exodus 21:11), and because of the superfluity they made from it two going-outs: וְיָצְאָה חִנָּם, and וְיָצְאָה אֵין כָּסֶף — and from it they derived a going-out in maidenhood, and in womanhood for one who has no maidenhood, namely an aylonit (one who has no signs of puberty), as is stated at the beginning of Kiddushin (4a).\nAnd the way of the peshat in this pericope is that it says: <b>If a man sells his daughter</b> — his young daughter — <b>as a maidservant, she shall not go out as the slaves go out</b> — those mentioned in the seventh year (Exodus 21:2) and in the Jubilee (Leviticus 25:10) — rather, the father may not remove her from his house forever if the girl pleases him and she is taken in lovingkindness before him, but he shall take her for himself as a wife as he wishes. And <b>if she is evil in the eyes of her master, who designated her for himself as a wife</b> — for one who acquires a daughter of Israel to take her as his wife acquires her for that purpose, and behold she is designated for him from the outset — <b>and now if her master does not desire her, and the aforementioned father has redeemed her</b>, for it is forbidden for him to leave her any longer in the master's hand from the time he says, \"I did not desire to take her.\" <b>And he shall not have power to sell her to a foreign people when he deals deceitfully with her</b>, for this is treachery — that a person sells his daughter to anyone other than one who is able to marry her. Alternatively, the reason is that everyone who sells his daughter deals deceitfully with her."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:31.895716028Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}